===Etymology 1===
Modification of {{inh|en|enm|tortou}}, {{m|enm|tortu}}, from {{der|en|fro|tortüe}} (under the influence of {{noncog|enm|turtel}}, {{m|enm|turtur|t=turtledove}}, see Etymology 2 below), from {{der|en|ML.|tortuca}} (compare {{cog|es|tortuga}}), the same source of {{m|en|tortoise}} (see there for more). Displaced native {{noncog|ang|byrdling}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|tortle}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|zoology|US|Canada}} Any land or marine [[reptile]] of the order [[Testudines]], characterised by a protective [[shell]] enclosing its body. See also '''{{l|en|tortoise}}'''.
#: {{syn|en|shellpad|q=obsolete|shield-toad|q2=archaic}}
# {{lb|en|zoology|Australia|British|specifically}} A marine [[reptile]] of that order.
#: {{syn|en|sea turtle}}
# {{lb|en|military|historical}} An [[Ancient Rome|Ancient Roman]] attack method, where the [[shield]]s held by the soldiers hide them, not only left, right, front and back, but also from above.
#: {{syn|en|testudo}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A type of [[robot]] having a [[domed]] case (and so resembling the reptile), used in education, especially for making [[line drawing]]s by means of a [[computer program]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} An [[on-screen]] [[cursor]] that serves the same function as a turtle for drawing.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Brian Harvey|title=Computer Science Logo Style: Symbolic computing|passage=Depending on which version of Logo you have, the '''turtle''' may look like an actual animal with a head and four legs or — as in Berkeley Logo — it may be represented as a triangle.}}
# {{lb|en|printing|historical}} The curved [[plate]] in which the [[form]] is held in a type-revolving cylinder press.
# {{lb|en|comptheory}} A small [[element]] towards the end of a [[list]] of items to be [[bubble sort]]ed, and thus tending to take a long time to be swapped into its correct position. Compare {{m|en|rabbit}}.
# {{lb|en|dance}} A [[breakdancing]] [[move]] consisting of a [[float]] during which the dancer's weight shifts from one hand to the other, producing rotation or a circular "walk".
# {{lb|en|television}} A low [[stand]] for a [[lamp]] etc.
#* Alan Bermingham, ''Location Lighting for Television''
#*: Using an appropriate '''turtle''' allows the full range of pan and tilt adjustments on the luminaire and avoids possible heat damage to floor coverings.

